From 456c3bd7dae07c580512076fad15b7bf5bbd1b07 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Akisaira <akisaira@outlook.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 01:42:10 +1100
Subject: [PATCH] libcamera: software_isp: Correct phase detection pixels
 before debayering

The ov13b10 has partly masked phase detection pixels at 16 blue sites of
every 32x32 block in its full-size mode, about 1.25 times as bright as
the other blue pixels. Debayered as image pixels they leave a 32-pixel
grid of blue dots in the picture.

Record the positions in the sensor properties database. They are given
per sensor transform: sensors whose flips keep the Bayer order move the
readout window by a pixel in a direction of their own, so flipping moves
the positions by more than a mirror image. The ov13b10's are known for
both flips, as mounted in the Huawei MateBook E Go.

The software ISP corrects only frames of the whole active area at full
resolution, as the driver's analogue crop can't be trusted to tell a
binned mode, and only with a transform the positions are known for. The
ov13b10's 4160x3120 and 4160x2340 crop modes stay uncorrected. The CPU
debayer replaces each pixel in its copy of the input line with the mean
of the two same colour pixels two away, along the direction where they
differ less. The input
buffer is left alone, as a raw stream may share it, and line copies are
always made while there is something to correct. Only CSI-2 packed
10-bit data is handled.

On the tablet, in the 4208x3120 mode, the blue channel's 32-pixel
fixed pattern drops from 5.1 to 0.9 (RMS of the high-passed image
averaged over 32x32 blocks; red, without such pixels, is at 0.7), with
the frame rate unchanged at 30 fps.

Signed-off-by: Akisaira <akisaira@outlook.com>
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

Origin: local
Upstream-Status: Pending
